---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '@/components/PageHero.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { YOUTUBE } from '@/lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About NeoKemetAI"
  description="About NeoKemetAI: our approach to ancient Egyptian history, the chronology we use, our sources and how AI reconstructions are made."
>
  <PageHero kicker="About" title="Ancient Egypt, reconstructed" glyph="𓋹">
    <Breadcrumbs slot="above" items={[{ label: 'About', href: '/about/' }]} />
  </PageHero>

  <div class="container section prose">
    <p>
      NeoKemetAI is the companion platform to the <a href={YOUTUBE.url} rel="noopener">NeoKemet YouTube channel</a>. It
      brings together the history behind our films: the thirty dynasties, the pharaohs, and the monuments, tombs and objects they
      left behind.
    </p>

    <h2>Chronology</h2>
    <p>
      Dates follow the conventional chronology of <em>The Oxford History of Ancient Egypt</em> (ed. Ian Shaw, 2000). Before
      664 BCE every date is approximate, often by decades, and scholars disagree about the length of several reigns. Where
      dynasties overlap, rival royal houses ruled different parts of the country at the same time.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Ian Shaw (ed.), <em>The Oxford History of Ancient Egypt</em>, Oxford University Press.</li>
      <li>Aidan Dodson &amp; Dyan Hilton, <em>The Complete Royal Families of Ancient Egypt</em>, Thames &amp; Hudson.</li>
      <li>Nicholas Reeves &amp; Richard H. Wilkinson, <em>The Complete Valley of the Kings</em>, Thames &amp; Hudson.</li>
      <li>Collections and catalogue records of the Egyptian Museum (Cairo), the Grand Egyptian Museum, the British Museum, the Metropolitan Museum of Art and the Neues Museum.</li>
      <li>Theban Mapping Project and the ISAC (formerly Oriental Institute), University of Chicago.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How our visuals are made</h2>
    <p>
      The imagery in NeoKemet films is AI-assisted archaeological reconstruction. It is an interpretation based on excavated
      evidence and museum objects, not photographs or footage. Narration is written originally for the channel, and each
      video's YouTube description lists its sources.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
